# aeneid-024 draft: each unit names where it ENDS (line, closing text); t is cut from the source by aeneid_lib.cut.
TITLE = "Dido saucia. Anna. Iunonis consilium"
TITLE_EN = "Dido's Wound, Anna's Counsel and Juno's Offer — Aeneid IV.1–104"
PART = "Dido's Wound, Anna's Counsel and Juno's Offer (IV.1–104)"
ABOUT = ("Book IV opens on the queen of Carthage. Dido is wounded by love for Aeneas and cannot sleep: his courage, his lineage, his words and his face keep returning to her. At dawn she confesses it to her sister Anna. She believes he is of divine stock; she had sworn to take no second husband after Sychaeus was murdered; and yet this man alone has shaken her resolve. She would sooner be swallowed by the earth than break faith with Shame. Anna answers that she is wasting her youth for the dead, that the hostile peoples of Africa and her brother's threats from Tyre make an ally against them a gift, and that the gods have surely brought the Trojans here. She tells Dido to sacrifice, to be hospitable, and to keep the guest from sailing while winter holds the sea. Her words inflame Dido further. The two women make sacrifice, above all to Juno, guardian of marriage, but vows and shrines cannot cure the silent wound. Dido wanders the city like a wounded deer with the arrow still in her flank, makes Aeneas tell his tale again and again, and lets the building of Carthage stop. Juno sees it, and proposes a bargain to Venus with bitter irony: let the two of them rule both peoples together, Dido married to the Trojan and the Tyrians given as her dowry.")
U = [
(2, "igni.",
 "But the queen, by heavy care long since wounded,\nfeeds a wound in her veins, and is plucked at by a blind fire.",
 "But the queen, long since wounded by a heavy care, nurses the hurt in her veins and is consumed by a hidden fire.",
 "The book opens on Dido, whom the reader last saw at the end of Book I falling in love; this line also begins one of Greenough's paragraphs. 'regina' is the subject; 'saucia' (wounded) agrees with it; 'gravi cura' is an ablative of cause, 'by heavy care'. 'iamdudum' is an adverb, 'for a long time now'. Love is a wound and a fire, the two images that run through the whole book. 'caeco igni' — 'blind' means hidden, unseen. 'carpitur' is passive: she is plucked at, worn away. 'volnus' is Greenough's spelling of 'vulnus'."),
(4, "honos:",
 "Much of the man's courage to her mind, and much\nof his race's honour runs back:",
 "Again and again the man's courage comes back to her mind, and the great honour of his race:",
 "'multa… virtus' and 'multus… honos' — each adjective agrees with its noun and means 'much' (an abundance of); 'recursat' is a frequentative of 'recurro', 'keeps running back', with one verb serving both subjects. 'animo' is 'to her mind'. 'viri' is Aeneas, not named. The colon introduces what is meant by this: his looks and his words stay with her."),
(5, "quietem.",
 "cling, fixed in her breast, his looks\nand words, nor does care give calm rest to her limbs.",
 "His looks and his words stay fixed in her heart, and care will not give her limbs peaceful rest.",
 "'haerent' is plural with two subjects, 'voltus' (Greenough's spelling of 'vultus', looks, features) and 'verba'; 'infixi' agrees with them. 'dat' is singular with its own subject 'cura'. 'nec' here means 'and not'. 'membris' is dative, 'to her limbs'."),
(8, "sororem:",
 "The next dawn was travelling the lands with Phoebus's lamp,\nand Aurora from the sky had moved away the dewy shade,\nwhen thus her one-souled sister she addresses, ill in her mind:",
 "The next Dawn was lighting the earth with the lamp of Phoebus and had swept the damp shadow from the sky, when, scarcely in her right mind, she speaks thus to the sister who shares her heart:",
 "A new paragraph, and a time-marker in the epic manner: 'Postera' (the following) goes with 'Aurora' in the next line, Dawn personified. 'Phoebea lampade' — the lamp of Phoebus, the Sun. 'lustrabat' and 'dimoverat' are imperfect and pluperfect, and 'cum' with the present 'adloquitur' means 'when': the main action arrives in the last line. 'umentem' is 'humentem', damp, dewy. 'unanimam' (one-souled) is the first word of the book to show how close the sisters are. 'male sana' is 'ill in mind', hardly sane."),
(9, "terrent!",
 "“Anna, sister, what sleepless nights terrify me in suspense!",
 "“Anna, my sister, what sleepless terrors hold me in dread suspense!",
 "Dido's speech opens (the marks run to line 29). 'quae' is the exclamatory adjective 'what', agreeing with 'insomnia', here troubled wakeful nights or visions. 'suspensam' agrees with 'me': hanging, uncertain."),
(11, "armis!",
 "Who this new guest has come to our dwellings,\nwhat a man bearing himself in his face, how brave in heart and arms!",
 "Who is this stranger who has come to our home — what a bearing in his face, what a brave heart, what prowess in arms!",
 "She does not name Aeneas. 'Quis… hic hospes' is the question; then the question turns to exclamation: 'quem' (what a man) and 'quam' (how). 'sese ore ferens' — carrying himself in his face; 'ore' is his countenance. 'successit' takes the dative 'sedibus', 'came into our dwellings'. 'hospes' is guest and stranger at once, the word that is always used of Aeneas in this book."),
(12, "deorum.",
 "I believe indeed — nor is the trust vain — his race to be of gods.",
 "I do believe — and my belief is no empty one — that he is of the race of the gods.",
 "'credo' takes an accusative and infinitive: 'genus esse deorum', 'that his race is of gods'. 'nec vana fides' is a parenthesis, 'and the belief is not empty'. Aeneas is in fact the son of Venus. 'deorum' is the ordinary genitive plural; compare the older 'divom' that Greenough prints at line 95."),
(13, "arguit:",
 "Degenerate spirits fear proves:",
 "Fear proves a spirit base-born:",
 "'degeneres animos' is the object of 'arguit' (proves, exposes), and 'degeneres' means 'unworthy of one's birth'. The thought is an argument: since he shows no fear, he cannot be of base stock."),
(14, "fatis!",
 "ah, by what fates he\ntossed!",
 "ah, what fates have driven him to and fro!",
 "'quibus… fatis' is an ablative of means with 'iactatus', exclamatory: 'by what fates!' 'iactatus' is the perfect participle passive, 'tossed about', agreeing with 'ille' with 'est' left out. The word echoes the poem's own opening, where Aeneas is 'much tossed' ('iactatus')."),
(14, "canebat!",
 "What wars endured to the end he was telling!",
 "What wars he told of, fought through to the end!",
 "'exhausta' is literally 'drained to the last drop', so endured right through. 'canebat' is the imperfect of 'cano', he used to tell, he kept telling: the reference is to the tale of Troy told in Books II–III."),
(17, "fefellit;",
 "If to me not in my mind fixed and unmoved it were settled,\nthat to no one with a bond I should wish to join myself in marriage,\nafter my first love by death has deceived, cheated me;",
 "If it were not settled in my mind, fixed and unshaken, that I would join myself to no one in the bond of marriage, since my first love failed me by death and left me cheated,",
 "A long conditional that finishes at line 19. 'sederet' is imperfect subjunctive: a condition contrary to fact, 'if it were not settled'. Its subject is the whole clause 'ne cui… vellem', 'that I should wish to join myself to no one'; 'cui' is 'alicui' after 'ne'. 'vinclo iugali' is a bond of the yoke, marriage. 'postquam primus amor… fefellit' — 'fefellit' has 'me' for its object, with 'deceptam' agreeing: 'cheated me and left me deceived'. The first love is Sychaeus, her husband."),
(19, "culpae.",
 "if it had not been wearisome — of the marriage-chamber and the torch —\nto this one failing perhaps I was able to yield.",
 "and if I had not become weary of the marriage bed and its torch, then perhaps I could have yielded to this one weakness.",
 "'pertaesum fuisset' is impersonal, 'it had wearied (me)', with a genitive of the thing: 'thalami taedaeque', of the bridal chamber and the wedding torch. The conclusion is the indicative 'potui', 'I could have' (a verb of ability often stands in the indicative where English would say 'would'). 'succumbere' takes the dative 'culpae'. Dido already calls it a 'fault'."),
(23, "impulit:",
 "Anna — for I shall confess — after the fates of wretched Sychaeus,\nmy husband, and the household gods spattered by a brother's slaughter,\nthis man alone has bent my feelings, and my wavering mind\nhas pushed:",
 "Anna, I will confess it: since the death of my unhappy husband Sychaeus, and the household gods spattered by my brother's murder, this man alone has swayed my feelings and given my faltering heart its push:",
 "'fatebor enim' is a parenthesis, 'for I will confess'. 'miseri… Sychaei coniugis' — the husband is Sychaeus, murdered by Dido's brother Pygmalion at the altar (the story is told in Book I). 'fata' is his fate, his death. 'Penatis' is Greenough's accusative plural in '-is' (you'll see this constantly: 'omnis', 'montis'): the household gods, spattered with blood because the murder was done at the altar. 'solus hic' is the emphatic subject. 'animum labantem impulit' — a tottering mind given the push that sends it over."),
(23, "flammae.",
 "I acknowledge the spoor of an old flame.",
 "I know the marks that an old fire left on me.",
 "'adgnosco' is Greenough's spelling of 'agnosco' (the Latin Library has the familiar form; same word). It is a compound of 'ad' and 'gnosco' (learn to know), so 'know again, acknowledge as one's own'. 'vestigia' means footprints, the tracks that a hunter follows; 'veteris flammae' is the old love, for Sychaeus. Love as flame is the book's metaphor and the same image appears in line 2; footprints prepare the reader for the hunted doe at lines 69–73."),
(27, "resolvo.",
 "But for me either earth, I would wish, first open to its depths,\nor the Father almighty drive me with his thunderbolt to the shades,\nthe pale shades of Erebus and deep night,\nbefore, Shame, I violate you, or loosen your laws.",
 "But before I violate you, Shame, or break your laws, I pray that the earth may first open deep beneath me, or the Almighty Father drive me with his thunderbolt down to the shades, the pale shades of Erebus and bottomless night.",
 "'optem' (I would wish) is a parenthesis; the wishes themselves are the subjunctives 'dehiscat' and 'adigat', 'let the earth gape, let the Father drive'. 'mihi' is a dative of interest, 'for me'. 'Pater omnipotens' is Jupiter. 'pallentis' is the accusative plural in '-is', agreeing with 'umbras'. Erebus is the dark of the underworld. The Latin Library has 'Erebo' (ablative: the shades 'in/to Erebus'), against Greenough's genitive 'Erebi'; either way the shades are of the dark world below. 'Pudor' is personified Shame, here the sense of fidelity to the dead husband. 'ante… quam' is followed by the indicative 'violo' and 'resolvo', which are present in Greenough's text. 'iura resolvo' — loosen, undo, her obligations."),
(29, "abstulit;",
 "That man my loves, who first joined me to himself,\ncarried off;",
 "He who first joined me to himself took my love away;",
 "'Ille' is Sychaeus. 'amores' is a plural of the abstract (love, affection). The relative clause 'primus qui me sibi iunxit' is placed inside the main clause, between 'meos' and 'amores'."),
(29, "sepulchro.”",
 "let him have them with himself and keep them in the tomb.”",
 "let him keep it with him, and guard it in the grave.”",
 "'habeat servetque' are jussive subjunctives ('let him have and keep'). 'ille' is repeated for emphasis. 'sepulchro' is Greenough's spelling (the Latin Library has 'sepulcro'). Dido's speech ends here."),
(30, "obortis.",
 "So having spoken, her lap with tears she filled, arisen.",
 "So she spoke, and filled the fold of her gown with welling tears.",
 "'effata' is the perfect participle of the deponent 'effor', and so active in sense, 'having spoken'. 'sinum' is the fold of the dress over the breast. 'obortis' (ablative with 'lacrimis') means 'rising up, welling'."),
(31, "refert:",
 "Anna replies:",
 "Anna answers:",
 "A new paragraph. 'refert' is a historic present introducing the reply. Anna's speech marks run to line 53."),
(33, "noris?",
 "“O more dear than light to your sister,\nwill you alone, mourning, waste away in perpetual youth,\nand neither sweet children nor Venus's rewards know?",
 "“O sister, dearer to me than the light of day, will you waste your youth alone in unending grief, and never know sweet children or the gifts of Venus?",
 "'luce' is an ablative of comparison with 'magis dilecta': dearer than light. 'sorori' is the dative with 'dilecta', 'to your sister', Anna speaking of herself in the third person. 'solane' is 'sola' with the question-particle '-ne'. 'carpere' is the infinitive in a question of protest: 'will you waste away?' 'noris' is the contracted form of 'noveris' (perfect subjunctive of 'nosco'). 'Veneris praemia' is love and its consequences."),
(34, "sepultos?",
 "That ashes or Shades do you believe care about, buried?",
 "Do you think the ashes or the buried spirits care for that?",
 "'Id' is the object of 'curare'. 'Manis' is the accusative plural in '-is' (Manes, the spirits of the dead), and 'sepultos' is 'buried', agreeing with it. The sarcasm is that the dead are indifferent to Dido's faithfulness."),
(35, "Esto:",
 "Be it so:",
 "Granted:",
 "'esto' is the future imperative of 'sum': 'let it be so'; Anna concedes the point before a new objection."),
(38, "alit:",
 "sick at heart, no suitors once bent you,\nnot in Libya, not before in Tyre; Iarbas spurned,\nand other chieftains, whom Africa, land\nrich in triumphs, nourishes:",
 "No suitor ever swayed you in your grief, neither in Libya nor earlier in Tyre; Iarbas was spurned, and the other chieftains whom Africa, a land rich in triumphs, breeds:",
 "'aegram' agrees with the 'te' left understood, 'you in your sickness of heart'. 'nulli… mariti' — 'maritus' is a husband, here a would-be husband, a suitor. 'flexere' is 'flexerunt' (the ending '-ere' for '-erunt'; you'll see it constantly). 'Libyae' and 'Tyro' are 'in Libya' and 'in Tyre'. 'despectus Iarbas' has 'est' omitted: Iarbas was spurned, a king of the neighbouring peoples whose name returns later in the book. 'triumphis dives' — rich in triumphs; the ablative goes with 'dives'."),
(38, "amori?",
 "to a pleasing love even will you fight?",
 "will you really struggle even against a love that pleases you?",
 "'placito… amori' is the dative after 'pugnabis' (fight against); 'placito' is the participle of 'placeo', 'that pleases'. The '-ne' makes the question; 'etiam' is 'even'."),
(39, "arvis?",
 "Nor does it come into your mind, in whose fields you have settled?",
 "Does it not cross your mind in whose lands you have settled?",
 "'in mentem venit' is the idiom 'it comes to mind'. 'quorum… arvis' is an indirect question: 'in whose fields'. 'consederis' is perfect subjunctive of 'consido'. The peoples who surround the city are listed next."),
(41, "Syrtis;",
 "On this side Gaetulian cities, a race unconquerable in war,\nand the unbridled Numidians surround you, and the inhospitable Syrtis;",
 "On this side the Gaetulian cities, a race unconquerable in war, encircle you, with the unbridled Numidians and the harbourless Syrtis;",
 "'cingunt' is one verb serving three subjects: the Gaetulian cities, the Numidians, and the Syrtis, the notorious shoals off the African coast. 'genus insuperabile bello' is in apposition: the Gaetulians are a race unconquerable 'in war' (ablative). 'infreni' is 'unbridled'. 'hinc… hinc' in the next line means 'on this side… on that'."),
(43, "Barcaei.",
 "on that side a region deserted by thirst, and far and wide raging\nthe Barcaeans.",
 "on the other, a region desolate with drought, and the Barcaeans raging far and wide.",
 "'siti' is the ablative of 'sitis', thirst: a land left empty by drought. 'late' with 'furentes', raging far and wide. The Barcaeans are a people of Barca in Libya."),
(44, "minas?",
 "Why should I mention wars from Tyre arising,\nand your brother's threats?",
 "Why should I speak of the war now rising out of Tyre, and of your brother's threats?",
 "'Quid… dicam' is a deliberative subjunctive: 'what am I to say of…?' 'Tyro' is ablative, 'out of Tyre'; the brother is Pygmalion, who murdered Sychaeus. Line 44 stops after 'germanique minas?': it is one of the unfinished half-lines Virgil left in the poem, and the sense is complete at the break."),
(46, "carinas.",
 "With gods as auspices, I think indeed, and with Juno favourable,\nthis course the Ilian keels by the wind have held.",
 "I truly believe it was by the gods' auspices, and with Juno's favour, that the Trojan ships held to this course before the wind.",
 "'Dis auspicibus' and 'Iunone secunda' are ablative absolutes with no participle (the verb 'sum' has no present participle): 'the gods being the guides, Juno being favourable'. 'reor' is the deponent 'I think'. 'tenuisse' is the infinitive in accusative and infinitive: the ships kept this course. 'Iliacas' is Trojan (Ilium). 'secunda' literally means 'following', hence favouring. Juno is in fact the Trojans' bitter enemy, and Anna is wrong; the irony is Virgil's."),
(48, "tali!",
 "What city, sister, this one will you see, what kingdoms to rise\nfrom such a marriage!",
 "Sister, what a city you will see here, and what kingdoms rising, with such a marriage!",
 "'Quam urbem… cernes' is exclamatory: 'what a city!'; 'quae… regna' is another exclamation, and 'cernes' takes the accusative and infinitive 'regna surgere', 'kingdoms rising'. 'coniugio tali' — an ablative of means or circumstance: 'by such a union'. 'coniugio' has a consonantal i in the middle: con-yu-gio."),
(49, "rebus!",
 "With Teucrians' arms accompanying,\nPunic glory itself with how great deeds will lift up!",
 "With Trojan arms beside her own, how high will Punic glory raise itself, and by what mighty deeds!",
 "'Teucrum' is the old genitive plural for 'Teucrorum' (you'll see this constantly: 'virum', 'divom'). 'Teucri' is Trojans, from their early king Teucer. 'comitantibus armis' is an ablative absolute. 'Punica gloria' is Carthaginian glory (Punic is from the Latin for Phoenician). 'se attollet' is 'will raise itself'. 'quantis rebus' is an ablative of means with exclamatory 'quantis': by what great deeds. The Carthage of the future that Anna imagines is one the Romans would remember as their great enemy."),
(53, "caelum.”",
 "You only ask the gods for favour, and the sacrifices having been favourable,\nindulge hospitality, and weave causes of delaying,\nwhile on the sea rages out the storm and rainy Orion,\nand the ships are battered, while the sky is not to be handled.”",
 "Only beg the gods for their favour, and when the offerings have won good omens, give yourself to hospitality and weave pretexts for delay, while winter rages on the sea, and rainy Orion, and the ships lie battered, while the sky cannot be dealt with.”",
 "'modo' with an imperative means 'only'. 'sacris litatis' is an ablative absolute: 'litare' is to sacrifice with favourable omens, so 'the sacrifices having been accepted'. 'indulge hospitio' — give way to, 'indulge in', with the dative. 'morandi' is the gerund in the genitive, 'of delaying'. 'dum… desaevit' is a present indicative with 'dum', 'while'. 'hiemps' is Greenough's spelling of 'hiems', winter, storm. Orion's setting brought storms at sea. 'non tractabile' is 'not to be handled, unmanageable'. Anna's speech ends here."),
(55, "pudorem.",
 "By these words her kindled mind she inflamed with love,\nand hope she gave to her doubtful mind, and loosed her shame.",
 "With these words she set Dido's kindled heart ablaze with love; she gave hope to a wavering mind and loosed the hold of shame.",
 "A new paragraph. The subject is Anna, unnamed. Greenough prints 'incensum animum inflammavit amore': 'she inflamed the already kindled mind'. The Latin Library has instead 'impenso animum flammavit amore', 'she set the mind aflame with intense love'; the sense differs only in emphasis, whether the mind was already burning or the love is intense. 'dubiae menti' is a dative, 'to a doubting mind'. 'solvit pudorem' — loosed her shame, undid the scruple Dido had named 'Pudor' at line 27."),
(57, "exquirunt;",
 "First the shrines they approach, and peace through the altars\nthey seek;",
 "First they go to the shrines and seek the gods' goodwill at the altars;",
 "The subject, 'they', is the two sisters, not named. 'adeunt' and 'exquirunt' are present tenses in the narrative. 'pacem' here is the favour of the gods, which sacrifice seeks. 'principio' is an adverbial ablative, 'at the start'."),
(59, "curae.",
 "they slay chosen sheep according to custom\nto law-giving Ceres and to Phoebus and to Father Lyaeus,\nto Juno before all, to whom the bonds of marriage are a care.",
 "they slay chosen sheep, as custom requires, to Ceres the lawgiver, to Phoebus, and to Father Lyaeus, and above all to Juno, whose care is the bonds of marriage.",
 "'bidentis' is the accusative plural in '-is' of 'bidens', a sheep of two years, the usual sacrificial animal. 'de more' is 'according to custom'. 'legiferae Cereri' — Ceres the lawgiver, patron of settled civic life. 'Lyaeus' is a name of Bacchus. 'ante omnis' is 'before all' (accusative plural in '-is'). 'cui vincla iugalia curae' is a double dative with 'est' left out: 'to whom the marriage bonds are a care'. Juno, patron of marriage, is the very goddess who will be at work against the Trojans."),
(64, "exta.",
 "She herself, holding a bowl in her right hand, fairest Dido,\nof a shining cow between the middle horns pours,\nor before the faces of the gods to the fat altars paces,\nand renews the day with gifts, and, of the beasts opened\nbreasts gazing, the breathing entrails consults.",
 "Dido herself, loveliest of women, holds the bowl in her right hand and pours the wine between the horns of a gleaming white cow, or walks before the gods' faces to the rich altars; she renews the day with gifts, and, bending eagerly over the opened breasts of the victims, consults the quivering entrails.",
 "A five-line sentence of ritual. 'patera' is the shallow libation bowl. 'candentis vaccae' is genitive, 'of a gleaming white cow'; 'media inter cornua' — 'inter' placed after its noun, between the horns. 'deum' is 'deorum' (the old short genitive plural). 'pinguis' is the accusative plural in '-is', agreeing with 'aras'. 'instaurat diem donis' — 'instaurat' is to renew, to do again: she begins the day again with fresh gifts. 'pecudum reclusis pectoribus' is an ablative absolute, 'the breasts of the beasts having been opened'. 'inhians' is gaping at, gazing eagerly. 'spirantia exta' — the entrails still seem to breathe; they were read for omens."),
(65, "mentes!",
 "Alas, minds ignorant of seers!",
 "Alas for minds that are blind to what the seers know!",
 "A new paragraph, and the narrator breaks in with a comment of his own. 'vatum' is the genitive plural of 'vates', seer or prophet; 'ignarae' (ignorant) takes the genitive. The reader knows how the story ends and Dido's priests cannot."),
(66, "iuvant?",
 "what vows a raging woman,\nwhat shrines help?",
 "What can vows do for a woman in her frenzy, what can shrines?",
 "'iuvant' takes 'furentem' (the raving Dido) as its object, with 'quid' an adverbial 'what good?'. The rhetorical question is the narrator's. 'delubra' is the word for shrines at line 56."),
(67, "volnus.",
 "Eats a soft flame the marrow meanwhile,\nand silent lives under her breast the wound.",
 "Meanwhile a gentle flame eats at her marrow, and the silent wound lives on beneath her heart.",
 "'Est' here is not 'is' but a form of 'edo', to eat: 'edit', 'eats'. 'mollis' can be read with 'flamma' (a soft flame) or with 'medullas' (soft marrow; accusative plural in '-is'); the sense is a love that eats into her without outward sign. 'tacitum… volnus' picks up 'volnus' of line 2: the poem has come round to its opening image."),
(72, "nescius;",
 "Burns unhappy Dido, and through all the city\nwanders raging, like a doe struck by an arrow,\nwhom from afar unwary among Cretan woods a shepherd\nhas pierced, pursuing with darts, and has left the winged iron\nunknowing;",
 "Unhappy Dido burns, and wanders raging through the whole city, like a doe struck by an arrow, which a shepherd, chasing her with his darts, has pierced from afar as she was unwary in the woods of Crete, and left the winged iron in her, not knowing it;",
 "'Uritur' is passive of 'uro', 'is burned', so 'she burns'. The simile begins at 'qualis': 'like a doe'. 'coniecta sagitta' is 'struck by an arrow' (ablative of means). 'quam' is the doe, object of 'fixit'. 'nemora inter' — 'inter' after its noun. 'Cresia' is Cretan (Crete was famed for archers). 'pastor agens telis' — a shepherd hunting her with darts. 'volatile ferrum' is the arrow, called the winged iron by metonymy of the material. 'nescius' agrees with 'pastor': he does not know he has hit her. The simile sets the image for what follows: the unaware hunter is Aeneas, the wounded doe is Dido."),
(73, "Dictaeos;",
 "she in flight woods and glades roams over\nof Dicte;",
 "she in her flight ranges through the woods and glades of Dicte;",
 "'illa' is the doe. 'peragrat' (ranges over) governs 'silvas saltusque'; 'Dictaeos' agrees with both, 'of Mount Dicte', a mountain in Crete."),
(73, "arundo.",
 "clings to her flank the deadly reed.",
 "the deadly shaft sticks fast in her side.",
 "'haeret lateri' — 'sticks to the flank', with the dative. 'arundo' is a reed, hence an arrow-shaft by metonymy (the Latin Library prints it 'harundo'; same word). 'letalis' is deadly."),
(75, "paratam;",
 "Now through the middle walls Aeneas with her she leads,\nand Sidonian wealth she shows and the city ready;",
 "Now she leads Aeneas with her through the heart of the city walls, and shows him the wealth of Sidon and the city made ready for him;",
 "'media… moenia' — 'media' agrees with 'moenia': through the midst of the walls. 'Aenean' is the Greek accusative singular, a form Virgil likes for Greek names. 'Sidonias' is Sidonian, which here simply means Phoenician or Carthaginian (Sidon was the neighbour of Tyre). 'paratam' is 'prepared, ready'. The present tenses start a run of repeated, habitual actions."),
(76, "resistit;",
 "she begins to speak out, and in the middle of the voice stops;",
 "she begins to speak, and halts in the middle of her words;",
 "'effari' is the infinitive of the deponent 'effor', to speak out. 'media in voce' — in the middle of the utterance, as her voice fails."),
(79, "ore.",
 "now the same, with the day sinking, banquets she seeks,\nand the Ilian toils again, out of her mind, to hear\nshe demands, and hangs again from the mouth of the teller.",
 "Now, as the day wanes, she seeks the same feasts again, and, beside herself, begs to hear once more of the toils of Troy, and hangs again on the lips of the one who tells them.",
 "'eadem… convivia' — the same banquets as before, 'eadem' is neuter plural. 'labente die' is an ablative absolute, 'as the day slips away'. 'demens' is out of her mind. 'exposcit' is an urgent demand. 'narrantis' is the present participle in the genitive, 'of the one telling'. 'pendet ab ore' — hangs from his lips. 'Iliacos' is Trojan, 'labores' toils."),
(83, "videtque;",
 "Afterwards, when they have parted, and the dim moon in turn\npresses out the light and the setting stars counsel sleep,\nalone in the empty house she grieves, and on the couch left behind\nshe lies, him absent, absent, she both hears and sees;",
 "Afterwards, when the guests have parted, and the dim moon in her turn dims the light and the setting stars invite sleep, she grieves alone in the empty house and lies upon the couch he left, hearing and seeing him, she who is absent, he who is absent;",
 "'ubi digressi' is 'when they have parted', with 'sunt' left out. 'obscura luna' — the moon, dim in her turn, 'vicissim'. 'suadent… somnos' — the stars persuade, counsel, sleep. 'sola… vacua' — the house is empty, she alone. 'stratisque relictis incubat' — she lies on the couch he left behind. 'illum absens absentem' is a pairing of the same word in two forms: she, absent, hears him, absent, a paradox on the figure of hearing and seeing what is not there."),
(85, "amorem.",
 "or in her lap Ascanius, by his father's image captured,\nshe holds, if she might deceive the unspeakable love.",
 "Or, captivated by the likeness of his father, she holds Ascanius in her lap, in case she might cheat the love she cannot speak of.",
 "'capta' agrees with Dido, the subject (not with Ascanius): she is caught by the boy's resemblance to his father. 'gremio' is an ablative of place, in her lap. 'infandum' is literally 'not to be spoken', unspeakable. 'si fallere possit' — 'if she might be able to deceive', that is, in the hope of cheating the love; the subjunctive expresses her hope."),
(88, "parant;",
 "The begun towers do not rise, not arms the youth\nexercises, nor harbours or bulwarks for war\nsafe prepare;",
 "The towers begun do not rise, the young men no longer drill in arms, and no one prepares harbours or bulwarks to make them safe in war;",
 "'coeptae turres' — the begun towers. 'adsurgunt' is Greenough's spelling, rise up. 'iuventus' is a collective singular (the young men). 'portusve aut propugnacula' — harbours and bulwarks; the verb 'parant' is plural, with 'they' (the Carthaginians) as its subject. 'tuta bello' is 'safe for war', with 'bello' ablative."),
(89, "caelo.",
 "hang the works interrupted, and the threats\nof walls huge and the engine levelled with the sky.",
 "The works hang broken off, the huge threatening walls, and the crane raised to match the sky.",
 "'pendent opera interrupta' — the works hang interrupted, broken off. 'minae murorum ingentes' — the threats of the walls, an idiom for walls that loom threateningly. 'machina' is a hoisting engine or crane. 'aequata caelo' is 'made level with the sky' (dative). The picture is of a city stopped in mid-construction."),
(92, "dictis:",
 "Whom as soon as she perceived to be held by such a plague,\ndear wife of Jove, and no repute to stand in the way of the madness,\nwith such words approaches Venus Saturn's daughter:",
 "As soon as Jove's dear wife saw that Dido was gripped by such a plague, and that her good name stood no longer in the way of her madness, Saturn's daughter accosted Venus with these words:",
 "A new paragraph. 'Quam' is a connecting relative, 'her' (Dido), the first word of the sentence in place of 'et eam'. 'persensit' governs two accusatives and infinitives: 'teneri' (that she was held) and 'famam obstare' (that repute stood in the way). 'fama' is her good name or reputation. 'cara Iovis coniunx' and 'Saturnia' both mean Juno (Saturn was her father); 'cara' is ironic. 'adgreditur' is a historic present, 'accosts'."),
(95, "est!",
 "“Egregious praise indeed and ample spoils you bring back,\nyou and your boy, great and memorable divinity,\nif one woman by the guile of two gods is conquered!",
 "“A splendid glory, truly, and rich spoils you carry off, you and that boy of yours — a great and memorable power! — if one woman has been beaten by the trickery of two gods!",
 "Juno's speech opens (the marks run to line 104). She is speaking with sarcasm. 'refertis' is 'you bring back', as of a victory. 'tuque puerque tuus' — 'you and your boy', Venus and Cupid, who disguised himself as Ascanius in Book I so as to kindle Dido. 'magnum et memorabile numen' is bitterly in apposition. 'divom duorum' — 'divom' is the old genitive plural of 'divus', Greenough's spelling (the Latin Library prints it 'diuum'; same word), of two gods. 'victa est' is the perfect passive: she has been conquered."),
(97, "altae.",
 "Nor does it escape me that you, fearing, my walls\nhave held suspect, the houses of high Carthage.",
 "Nor does it escape me that you, in your fear, regarded my walls with suspicion, the homes of high Carthage.",
 "'Nec me adeo fallit' takes an accusative and infinitive: 'te… habuisse', that you held. 'veritam' is the perfect participle of the deponent 'vereor', 'fearing'. 'adeo' strengthens the negative: 'not indeed'. 'suspectas habuisse' — 'to have held suspect'. 'domos' is an accusative plural in apposition to 'moenia'. 'Karthaginis' is Greenough's spelling. Carthage is Juno's own city; she calls the walls 'nostra'."),
(98, "tanto?",
 "But what will be the limit, or to what contest now so great?",
 "But what limit will there be, or what is the point of so great a contest now?",
 "'quo… certamine tanto' is a question with the verb left out: to what end such a contest? The rhetorical question begins her offer."),
(100, "exercemus?",
 "Why not rather peace eternal and pledged marriage\nlet us work at?",
 "Why do we not rather set about an everlasting peace and the marriage already agreed?",
 "'Quin' with the present indicative 'exercemus' makes an urging question: 'why do we not…?' 'exerceo' is to keep at work, to practise. 'hymenaeos' is the Greek accusative plural, wedding rites. 'pactos' is 'agreed', 'pledged'. Juno proposes what Venus supposedly wants and what Juno intends to turn to her own purposes."),
(100, "petisti:",
 "You have what with your whole mind you sought:",
 "You have what you sought with your whole heart:",
 "'Habes' is the indicative: you have, you have gained. 'petisti' is a contracted perfect for 'petivisti' (a contraction you will meet often). The colon introduces the proof."),
(101, "furorem.",
 "burns loving Dido, and has drawn through her bones the madness.",
 "Dido burns with love, and the frenzy has run through her very bones.",
 "'ardet amans' — 'amans' is the participle of 'amo', 'loving', in apposition to 'Dido'. 'traxit… furorem' — has drawn the madness through her bones, the verb a perfect. The words 'ardet' and 'furorem' pick up 'uritur' and 'furens' of lines 68–69."),
(103, "auspiciis;",
 "In common this people then, and with equal\nauspices let us rule;",
 "Let us then rule this people jointly, under equal auspices;",
 "'regamus' is a hortatory subjunctive, 'let us rule'. 'communem hunc populum' — framing 'ergo', 'then'. 'paribus auspiciis' is an ablative of attendant circumstances, with the same authority. 'auspicia' are the right to take omens and so to command."),
(104, "dextrae.”",
 "let it be allowed to serve a Phrygian husband,\nand as dowry to your right hand the Tyrians to entrust.”",
 "let her be permitted to serve a Phrygian husband, and to hand over the Tyrians to your keeping as a dowry.”",
 "'liceat' is a jussive subjunctive, 'let it be allowed', with the infinitives 'servire' (taking the dative 'Phrygio marito') and 'permittere' (with 'dextrae'). 'Phrygio' is Trojan; the Trojans are often called Phrygians in the Aeneid. 'dotalis' is the accusative plural in '-is' agreeing with 'Tyrios': 'as a dowry'. 'dextrae' is the right hand, here the power of Venus. The speech ends, and the part with it."),
]
